 ],
 "literal": "Filled-up moat, ruined rampart, / broken fortress wall, breached ancient town, / how will it become indeed, without restraining, / like thunder of falling rain, with resounding drums, / kings with swift horses, in the morning having come, our / tall-standing gate, they will circle around! / Without fighting they will not remain quiet, having exerted in war, / her brothers of killing strength and enmity, having raised / sharp spears like steel weapons, (for) the reddened kohl-adorned eyes / of the young woman with bracelet-adorned forearms, / and the beauty spots budded on her beautiful good chest!",
 "translation": "What will become of this ancient town with its filled-up moat, ruined ramparts, and breached fortress walls? Unstoppably, like the thunder of heavy rain, with resounding war drums, kings with swift horses will come in the morning and circle around our tall gate. They will not remain quiet without fighting. For the sake of the young woman with bracelet-adorned forearms, whose beautiful chest is budded with beauty spots and whose kohl-adorned eyes are as red as the sharp steel spears raised by her brothers of fierce, murderous strength, they will wage war.",
 "modern": "தூர்ந்துபோன அகழியும், இடிந்துபோன மதிலுறுப்புகளும், சிதைந்த கோட்டைச் சுவர்களும் கொண்ட இந்த பழைய ஊர் இனி என்னவாகுமோ? விடாமல் பெய்யும் மழையின் இடியைப் போல முழங்கும் முரசுகளுடன், வேகமான குதிரைகளைக் கொண்ட பகை வேந்தர்கள் காலையிலேயே வந்து நமது உயர்ந்த வாசலைச் சூழ்ந்து கொள்வார்கள். அவர்கள் போரிடாமல் சும்மா இருக்க மாட்டார்கள். வளையல்கள் அணிந்த முன்கைகளை உடைய அந்த இளம்பெண்ணின் அழகிய மார்பில் அரும்பிய தேமலுக்காகவும், அவளது கொலைவலிமை மிக்க அண்ணன்மார் ஏந்திய கூர்மையான வேல்களைப் போலச் சிவந்த அவளது மைதீட்டிய கண்களுக்காகவும், அவர்கள் போரிட்டு வருந்துவார்கள்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Puram genre, specifically the theme of Makaṭpāṟ Kāñci, which depicts the tragedy of refusing a daughter in marriage to powerful suitors. The speaker, a concerned townsperson, laments the impending destruction of their ancient town, whose defenses like the moat and ramparts are already in ruins. Great kings have arrived with swift horses and thunderous war drums, demanding the hand of a beautiful young woman. Her fierce brothers, proud warriors who wield sharp steel spears, refuse to give her away and are ready to fight to the death. The poet masterfully links the girl's beautiful, red-streaked eyes to the sharp, blood-stained spears of her brothers, showing that her beauty is the direct cause of the impending, destructive war.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
